                {
                  "text": "I'm working through the grid geometry, tracing a diagonal shot from the top-left corner down to the bottom-right corner of the 10x9 grid, then reflecting back along the anti-diagonal from that corner.\n\nAfter collecting the diagonal cells and adjusting for the boundary intersection, I check which of the grid's four corners share the background color versus a distinct one\u2014only the bottom-left corner (9,0) stands out as different, matching the isolated cell picked up outside the main diagonal.\n\nSince only that one corner differs, I select the corresponding transformed version of the diagonal pattern\u2014applying a horizontal mirror to align it with that corner's position.\n\nMirroring flips the diagonal so it now runs from (9,0) down through (0,8), with each row having exactly one filled cell stepping across columns as rows increase. Filling those cells with 1 and the rest with 8, I get a diagonal line running the opposite direction\u2014I want to double check this against the expected zigzag pattern from a smaller example to make sure the logic holds.\n\nTesting with a 10x3 grid: locating the diagonal intersection points, shooting a line down-left from the corner, and tracking the resulting cell sets through several derived quantities (heights, corners, shifts) to see if the construction reproduces the expected zigzag output.\n\nIt matches. Now redoing the other case: for a 10x9 grid, x9 is the diagonal plus point (9,7), and I check that llcorner using max row and min column doesn't actually correspond to a real element, so removing it changes nothing and height stays 10. Tracing through the subsequent shifts and unions, x23 ends up equal to x9 itself, and applying the hmirror with d=9 gives the reflected diagonal points.\n\nMapping out each row against its mirrored column, I get a proper zigzag pattern from row 0 through row 9, which confirms the transformation is working correctly.\n\n",
